    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "An additional source of genetic variance will appear in any line-cross derivative for which there is variation among individuals in the proportion of $ P_1 $ and $ P_2 $ genes. For example, in the $ F_2 $ generation there is a 50% probability of being $ P_1P_2 $ and 25% probabilities of being $ P_1P_1 $ or $ P_2P_2 $ at any locus. This variation is in contrast to that in the $ F_1 $ generation where all individuals are $ P_1P_2 $. Letting $ -\\alpha_i^c/2 $ and $ + \\alpha_i^c/2 $ be the mean additive effects of alleles at the $ i $th locus in the $ P_1 $ and $ P_2 $ lines, then the variance among $ F_2 $ individuals attributable to differences between parental lines at the locus is",
      "source_page": 243,
      "printed_page": 223,
