    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "A graphical comparison of the observed and predicted means and variances of human skin color (Figure 9.2) serves to illustrate two important points. First, due to the large standard errors of variance estimates, scaling tests based on variances are much less powerful than those based on means. Thus, while the preceding methodology can be generalized to compute the dominance and epistatic components of the segregational variance (Mather and Jinks 1982), the statistical reliability of such analyses is very low, and we will not pursue it.",
      "source_page": 247,
      "printed_page": 227,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter9:p027:b3"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
